.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_CONSTRUCT_SCOPED 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_construct_scoped \- declare, initialise, and auto-destroy a ccol_event_loop on scope exit
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_event_loop_construct_scoped(name, max_events_per_wait, num_lock_stripes,
                            num_reactor_threads);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares a ccol_event_loop variable called name, tags it with the
GCC and Clang cleanup attribute, and initialises it with
ccol_event_loop_create(). If the create fails, the macro calls
_ccol_fatal_err(). When the variable leaves its scope the loop is
destroyed automatically, with no explicit ccol_event_loop_destroy()
call, and that destroy shuts every reactor thread down, joins each one
and frees every registration.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
name                  Name of the variable for the ccol_event_loop handle.
max_events_per_wait   Size of the polling thread's event batch buffer,
                      from 1 to INT_MAX. It must also be small enough
                      that max_events_per_wait times the size of one
                      kernel event record (struct epoll_event on Linux,
                      struct kevent on FreeBSD) fits in a size_t.
num_lock_stripes      Number of independent lock stripes for the
                      registry of fds and entries, at least 1. With 1
                      there is a single shared lock.
num_reactor_threads   Number of background reactor threads, at least 1.
                      With 1, a single thread both polls and dispatches
                      the callbacks in line.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
A failure ends in _ccol_fatal_err().

If you count on no callback being able to reach something that lives in
this loop's scope, such as a mutex or a buffer that a callback's arg
points at, that must stay true up to and including the join at the end
of the scope. Holding up to an explicit ccol_event_loop_remove(3) call
before that point is not enough: a callback can outlive the return of
that call, as the notes of ccol_event_loop_remove(3) explain, and the
implicit shutdown at the end of the scope behaves the same way.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
{
    ccol_event_loop_construct_scoped(loop, 32, 1, 1);
    ccol_event_reg reg = ccol_event_loop_add(loop, ccol_selectable_from_fd(fd, ccol_select_read),
                                    handlers, NULL, NULL);
    /* ... */
}  /* the library shuts loop down here, joins every reactor thread,
      and destroys the loop */
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_event_loop_construct (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_declare_scoped (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_create (3),
.BR ccol_event_loop_remove (3)
